"# Chapter 14 — Publishing (there is no registry — you share source)

In the other dialect a module on your machine helps you; a module in the *registry* helps everyone, and publishing is a four-step ritual: sign, upload, tag, verify. **Motoi has no registry, no `module/sign`, no `module/publish`, no `module/keygen`, no version tags.** There is no `(module ...)` form to publish and no `(export ...)` list to sign. Each of those names yields `error: unbound symbol: ...`. This is a dialect-boundary chapter: it teaches what \"sharing code\" honestly means in a batteries-included Scheme that has no package manager.

## 14.1 The honest Motoi story: share the source

In Motoi, a piece of reusable code is a file of top-level `define`s that call the always-present standard library. You share it the way you share any Scheme: you hand someone the source. They read it, they run it, they call its verbs. There is no sign step, no upload step, no tag step — those are the other dialect's registry mechanics.

Here is a shareable piece of code — no `module`, no `export`, just definitions:

```scheme
(define (greet name) (string-append \"Hello, \" name \"!\"))
(define (farewell name) (string-append \"Goodbye, \" name \".\"))

(list (greet \"Alfred\") (farewell \"Alfred\"))
;; ⇒ (\"Hello, Alfred!\" \"Goodbye, Alfred.\")
```

That is the whole artifact. To \"publish\" it, you put the source somewhere others can read it. To \"consume\" it, they include the definitions and call `greet`. No import, because there is nothing to import from — the definitions are just there.

## 14.2 There is no sign step

The other dialect's `(module/sign 'manners :author \"a-friend\")` computes a content hash and signs it with a private key from `~/.motoi/keys/`. **Motoi binds no `module/sign` and no `module/keygen`** — both are `error: unbound symbol`. Motoi has no key management and no module artifacts to sign. The integrity of shared source, in Motoi, is the integrity of the file you were handed: you read it before you run it.

## 14.3 There is no upload step

`(module/publish 'manners :registry \"registry.motoi.dev\" :version \"1.0.0\")` pushed a signed module to a named registry in the other dialect. **Motoi has no `module/publish` and no registry** — the verb is unbound and there is no `registry.motoi.dev` to push to. Sharing is out-of-band: a repository, a gist, a message. The language has no opinion about the transport.

## 14.4 There are no version tags

Registries index modules by name and version, and pins like `^1.0.0` resolve against those tags. Motoi has no tags because it has nothing versioned to tag — its one standard library is simply the standard library, always present. There is no `:channel :beta`, no `latest` pointer, no resolve step. (Semantic versioning as an *engineering idea* is still worth knowing; Chapter 5 covers it as literacy, not as a Motoi mechanism.)

## 14.5 There is no verify step

`(module/fetch ...)` then `(module/verify ...)` round-tripped a published module in the other dialect. **Motoi binds neither** — `module/fetch` and `module/verify` are unbound. There is nothing fetched from a registry to verify against. The Motoi equivalent of \"does it round-trip\" is simply: run the source and see that it behaves.

## 14.6 Documenting what you share

The other dialect's reference generator reads `:doc` and `:example` off each `export`. Motoi has no `export`, but the durable habit survives: **document your verbs where a reader will see them.** In Motoi that is a comment above the `define`, or an entry in a book like this one. Good documentation is a property of the source you share, not of a registry field:

```scheme
;; greet: return a friendly greeting for a name.  (greet \"Alfred\") => \"Hello, Alfred!\"
(define (greet name) (string-append \"Hello, \" name \"!\"))

(greet \"Alfred\")
;; ⇒ \"Hello, Alfred!\"
```

The doc lives with the code. When you hand someone the file, the documentation goes with it — no separate metadata channel, no `:doc` field on an `export` that Motoi does not have.

## 14.7 The share habit

Even without a registry, the habit the other dialect taught survives: share small, share often. If you improve a helper, hand out the new source. If you fix a bug, share the fixed file. The \"cost\" is your attention, and attention paid frequently is cheaper than attention paid rarely. What changes in Motoi is only the mechanism: there is no publish command, just a file you pass along.

## 14.8 What Cortex can record

The other dialect logs a `module-published` event you can query. Motoi has no such event (nothing is published). But if you want to *note* that you shared a version of something, Cortex is real and you write the fact yourself:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'shared 'greet-v2)
(cortex/recall 'shared)
;; ⇒ a memory record whose :data is greet-v2
```

That is the honest Motoi shape of \"what did I ship\": a fact you chose to record, not a registry event.

## 14.9 Try it

Write two small verbs as top-level `define`s (section 14.1) and confirm they run. Add a documenting comment above one (section 14.6). Then hand the file to someone — that is publishing in Motoi. Confirm that `module/sign`, `module/publish`, and `module/verify` are all `unbound symbol` (section 14.2–14.5): there is no registry ritual, because there is no registry.

Chapter 15 covers forking and remixing in the other dialect — taking someone's module and making it your own — and, for Motoi, what remixing means when the unit you share is plain source. Turn the page.")
